WEBVTT

00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,320
Do your genes cause cancer, so let's jump into that right away.

00:00:06,320 --> 00:00:12,960
So there's very few cancers that we would say are caused by your genes.

00:00:12,960 --> 00:00:18,240
There are genetic mutations, especially you might have heard of the BRCA genes.

00:00:18,240 --> 00:00:20,320
Those are tumor suppressor genes.

00:00:20,320 --> 00:00:27,040
So a tumor suppressor gene is a gene that will turn on for a sake of a better word.

00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:33,120
If cancer is growing in your body and it will help your body kill the cancer.

00:00:33,120 --> 00:00:35,520
So tumor suppressor genes are very important.

00:00:35,520 --> 00:00:42,720
So defects in the tumor suppressor gene systems can contribute to cancer,

00:00:42,720 --> 00:00:46,080
but they'll never be a cause of cancer.

00:00:46,080 --> 00:00:53,520
So, for instance, if I have cancer growing in my breast and I have BRCA gene defects,

00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:57,520
so my tumor suppressor genes aren't going to be able to turn on to kill that cancer.

00:00:57,520 --> 00:01:00,000
You have other systems that are going to kill that cancer.

00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,840
So just because a person has defects in their tumor suppressor genes,

00:01:03,840 --> 00:01:06,480
certainly does not mean that they're going to get cancer.

00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:13,760
Now there are some other genes, especially with colon cancer, that if people with these specific

00:01:13,760 --> 00:01:18,960
genes have a much higher incidence of colon cancer, colorectal cancer.

00:01:19,680 --> 00:01:24,320
But for the most part, your genes are not your causative factor of your cancer.

00:01:25,680 --> 00:01:30,000
And the second part of this question is what about the MTHFR defect?

00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:36,160
Your MTHFR gene is a very important gene with detoxification, with methylation,

00:01:37,280 --> 00:01:40,240
but I certainly wouldn't say that that's a cause of cancer.

00:01:40,240 --> 00:01:48,560
Now genes can defects in certain pathways, especially pathways that are involved with detoxification.

00:01:48,560 --> 00:01:53,760
You could argue that they could be a secondary or tertiary cause of cancer,

00:01:53,760 --> 00:02:02,320
because if I'm exposed to toxins, and let's say two people are working the same job, spraying horrible

00:02:02,320 --> 00:02:11,840
chemicals on whatever, and I have a lot of defects in my detoxification pathways, and my co-worker

00:02:11,840 --> 00:02:19,360
doesn't, I'm going to be less readily able to detoxify those chemicals.

00:02:19,360 --> 00:02:25,920
So from a secondary standpoint, yes, I'm exposed to the same toxins as my co-worker.

00:02:25,920 --> 00:02:31,920
My co-worker is able to detox these out faster than I am.

00:02:31,920 --> 00:02:36,880
The chance that these chemicals are going to settle in my cells is much greater because of those

00:02:36,880 --> 00:02:43,760
defects in those pathways. The chance of that settling in my cell and disrupting the cell

00:02:43,760 --> 00:02:50,640
replication pathway is higher, and that's what cancer is. So cancer is, by definition,

00:02:50,640 --> 00:02:57,040
a cell that has its replication pathway affected in a way that the cell is going through rapid

00:02:57,040 --> 00:03:06,480
replication, and natural cell death, apoptosis, isn't taking place, and so that's what cancer is.

00:03:06,480 --> 00:03:12,880
So the cause of it really would, in this case, so this example would be toxicity,

00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:19,360
where are the genes the cause, well, is there is a secondary effect, or that I'm not able to detox

00:03:20,400 --> 00:03:27,120
quickly, and therefore the toxins that I'm exposed to aren't getting out of my body.

00:03:27,680 --> 00:03:33,440
So yes, in that case, yes, that's why when we look at a person's genes, that's part of all our

00:03:33,440 --> 00:03:38,560
playouts, and we think it's very important. Your detox pathways are one of the major things to

00:03:38,560 --> 00:03:47,680
look at, even more so than your tuber suppressor genes.

